    "owner_sha256": "71319d6462f3b56fd5287fbd70a94afac2d9f3e0aad55415aaadc2eb615a455b",
    "owner_mim_id": "MIM:0000383",
    "owner_primary_identifier": "CHEBI:33658",
    "owner_grounding": {
      "ontology_id": "CHEBI:33658",
      "ontology_label": "arene",
      "ontology_source": "CHEBI",
      "mapping_quality": "SYNONYM_MATCH",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "evidence_type": "LEXICAL_MATCH",
          "source": "kgm-metatraits",
          "notes": "Imported from kgm-metatraits via source-preset. source_id=kgm.metatraits.special:aromatic_hydrocarbon; "
        },
        {
          "evidence_type": "DATABASE_MATCH",
          "source": "MIM curation (#322)",
          "notes": "mapping_quality EXACT_MATCH -> BROAD_MATCH. CHEBI:33848 carries no structural definition, so it is a class rather than an individual, and the record's label 'Aromatic hydrocarbon' does not equal the term label 'polycyclic arene'. EXACT_MATCH therefore asserted an identity the pair does not have. Re-graded because the record is BROADER than the term: benzene is an aromatic hydrocarbon and is not a polycyclic arene, so narrowMatch would be backwards."
        },
        {
          "evidence_type": "MANUAL_CURATION",
          "source": "MIM curation (SSSOM delivery review)",
          "notes": "Regrounded CHEBI:33848 (kgmicrobe.compound:aromatic_hydrocarbon) -> CHEBI:33658 'arene'. CHEBI:33658 is defined as 'Any monocyclic or polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon', which is exactly what this record names; CHEBI:33848 'polycyclic arene' is a proper subset of it. The old shape asserted skos:broadMatch — the corpus's only one — and kg-microbe's consolidator reads narrowMatch and broadMatch identically as 'the MIM subject is NARROWER than the object', so it published this record as a CHILD of polycyclic arene: backwards, and chemically false. The record's own history carried both readings, the regrade calling it broader and the mint that followed calling it narrower. Grounding at the term that actually denotes it makes the mapping symmetric and true, which removes the disagreement rather than arguing about direction, and retires a registry mint the record never needed — a mint gives identity to a record NARROWER than every available term. Not a #322 class overstatement: both sides are the same class."
        }
      ]
    },
