  {
    "entry_id": "row-000807",
    "selection_entry_sha256": "5e231b8705e56de52f6bb95acee9f3a777ba92a0e55cb6e1768d81dc33aa1026",
    "candidate_position": 807,
    "before_position": 798,
    "class": "matched",
    "owner": "data/ingredients/mapped/Cobalamine.yaml",
    "owner_sha256": "23faceda75dce539d33f8690aae8d558bb3a744c73030d8bfddae9e5a702e992",
    "owner_mim_id": "MIM:0000731",
    "owner_primary_identifier": "CHEBI:30411",
    "owner_grounding": {
      "ontology_id": "CHEBI:30411",
      "ontology_label": "cobalamin",
      "ontology_source": "CHEBI",
      "mapping_quality": "EXACT_MATCH",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "evidence_type": "DATABASE_MATCH",
          "source": "CultureMech",
          "notes": "Imported from CultureMech pipeline, quality=DIRECT_MATCH"
        },
        {
          "evidence_type": "DATABASE_MATCH",
          "source": "MIM curation (#320/#319)",
          "notes": "identifier and ontology_id CHEBI:28911 -> CHEBI:30411. ChEBI's live definition of CHEBI:28911 is 'A cobalamin in which the central cobalt atom has an oxidation state of +3' — it specifies an oxidation state this record's label does not. 'Cobalamine' names the vitamer generically, so MAPPING_SEMANTICS §3 step 4 gives it the generic term CHEBI:30411 ('cobalamin'), which was unheld. The record's own CAS 18534-66-2 is registered by ChEBI against cob(I)alamin — a *third* oxidation state — which is the #320 conflict that surfaced this and is itself evidence the record was never specific to +3. Definition read from the live EBI OLS4 API, since #319 records that the local ChEBI build lacks definitions for some of these terms."
        },
        {
          "evidence_type": "DATABASE_MATCH",
          "source": "MIM curation (#138/#320)",
          "notes": "Cleared cas_rn, molecular_formula, inchi and smiles, and moved kg_microbe_node_id CHEBI:28911 -> CHEBI:30411. Every one of these encoded the +3 oxidation state this record does not assert. The formula/InChI/SMILES were written by AUTO_BACKFILL_CHEBI_CHEMISTRY on 2026-05-01 by copying CHEBI:28911 while the record was mapped there, so they were a transcription of the grounding rather than evidence for it — the InChI still ended '/q;+3/p-2'. The cas_rn 18534-66-2 is cob(I)alamin (KEGG C00853, ChEBI:15982, Gmelin 887721), a THIRD oxidation state, and came from a PubChem name lookup — the mechanism that also put CHES on Beef extract. CHEBI:30411 deliberately carries no formula, charge or mass because its oxidation state is unspecified, so a record grounded there must not carry structure fields that fix one. Leaving them would re-seed the same wrong inference on any future automated re-grading. Note the formula does not discriminate oxidation state in any case: ChEBI records the identical C62H88CoN13O14P for cob(II)alamin and cob(III)alamin, which differ only in the charge field."
        }
      ]
    },
