          "source": "MIM curation (#321)",
          "notes": "molecular_formula 'HO4P.2Na' -> 'HO4P.2Na.3H2O'. The recorded value described the anhydrous salt while the label names a hydrate, understating the mass by 18 g/mol per water. Corrected because the label states 3 water(s) of hydration, and the recorded formula was byte-identical to the anhydrous parent CHEBI:34683's ChEBI formula ('HO4P.2Na') — the mark of a value inherited from the parent rather than measured for this record. Water appended in ChEBI's own dot-separated notation."
        },
        {
          "evidence_type": "DATABASE_MATCH",
          "source": "MIM curation (#342)",
          "notes": "mapping_quality NARROW_MATCH -> CLOSE_MATCH, predicate skos:narrowMatch -> skos:closeMatch. narrowMatch asserts this record is *narrower in meaning* than CHEBI:34683 ('disodium hydrogenphosphate') — a subsumption ChEBI itself denies: it files hydrates under CHEBI:35505 'hydrate', never under the anhydrous compound, which is not an ancestor even transitively. A hydrate is a distinct substance containing the anhydrous form, not a specialization of it. closeMatch claims similarity without subsumption. The ontology_id and the record's own identifier are unchanged."
        },
        {
          "evidence_type": "CURATOR_JUDGMENT",
          "source": "MIM curation (#344)",
          "notes": "mediadive.compound:525 still carries the source label 'Na2HPO4 x 3 H2O', but no exact ontology term or CAS has been verified for that stated hydration count. CHEBI:34683 (disodium hydrogenphosphate) is retained only as the closest anhydrous parent while the source label keeps its local kgmicrobe.compound identity."
        }
      ]
